{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018RK8v5bFGgT2wJWTCkML7k","type":"tool_result","content":"./.ard-input/347/issue.md:13:Check only, never write: main's active ruleset requires a PR and has the ard App as an Always bypass actor (and nothing else unexpected), the App has no `workflows` permission, merge settings (`allow_rebase_merge` off when `ARD_VERSIONING` is on). Compare against a policy built into ard, not repo content. On drift print the exact fix (settings URL or `gh api` command). Rows that need admin read and lack it are `manual`. New `github.Reader` method for rulesets plus the fake.\n./docs/runbooks/release.md:18:- Repository variables: `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` (ard's App login, `<slug>[bot]`), `ARD_VERSIONING` (`on` for live), `RELEASE_APP_ID`, `RELEASE_BOT_NAME`, `RELEASE_BOT_EMAIL`.\n./docs/runbooks/release.md:89:2. Set `ARD_VERSIONING` to `on` (`gh variable set ARD_VERSIONING -R albertcbrown/ard -b on`).\n./docs/runbooks/release.md:102:- It does nothing until `ARD_VERSIONING` is `on` **and** the `release` environment holds the secret `TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN`. Without the token it logs a notice and stops.\n./docs/runbooks/release.md:131:Every run is a dry run until `ARD_VERSIONING` is `on`. Check the plan in a dry run's step summary, then set the variable. `workflow_dispatch` is a dry run unless `dry_run` is false; `max_tags` (default 20) caps the tags one run creates, so raise it after reading the plan when a backlog is larger.\n./docs/proposal.md:505:**`ard setup` applies `.ard/` to GitHub (decided, #303).** It reads the local `./.ard/` and warns when it differs from `origin/main`, which `ard serve` runs. With no flags it runs every check, then applies only if all of them are green; `--check` reports only (exit 2 on drift) and `--dry-run` routes writes through the effect layer. Each row is `ok`, `changed`, `drift` or `manual` with the exact fix. It **writes** only two things: the `ard:*` label catalogue (create and update, never delete; `ard labels sync` stays as an alias) and the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN`, derived from the App's own identity (`<slug>[bot]`), never from config. Variables need the operator's token (`gh auth token` or a PAT, #302), read once, held in memory and never logged, stored or passed to an agent or job. It **only checks**, against a policy built into ard rather than repo content, and prints the fix on drift: the shared permission check; `main`'s active ruleset requires a pull request and its bypass list is exactly the ard App in mode Always; the App has no `workflows` or `administration` permission; merge settings (`allow_rebase_merge` off once versioning is on); and `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` on `origin/main` against a copy embedded in ard (`ard setup --print-workflow` prints it for a human to commit). Setup never commits to the repository, `.github/` included, and never writes rulesets, bypass actors or App settings: a merged config change must not be able to loosen branch protection through an admin token. `ard setup --create-gh-app` (#350) creates the App itself with GitHub's manifest flow.\n./docs/proposal.md:564:- **Versioning ([#183](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/183)).** `version.yml` gives every first-parent merge to `main` an annotated tag `vX.Y.Z` and a notes-only release, oldest first. The bump comes from the merged PR's labels: `semver:major`, `semver:minor`, else patch; a direct push gets patch, and ard's own main-lane commits (committer `ARD_BOT_LOGIN`) are skipped. The seed tag is `v0.1.0`. The workflow is a dry run until the repository variable `ARD_VERSIONING` is `on`. `make build` (`git describe` limited to plain `vX.Y.Z` tags) and `go install ...@vX.Y.Z` (build info) both print the tag from `ard version`. `internal/versioncheck` tests the workflow's scripts. See `docs/runbooks/release.md`.\n./docs/proposal.md:565:- **Homebrew ([#177](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/177)).** `.github/workflows/release.yml` builds each new `v*` tag with GoReleaser (`.goreleaser.yaml`: darwin and linux, arm64 and amd64, CGO off, `-X main.version`), adds the archives and `checksums.txt` to the release `version.yml` created (`release.mode: keep-existing`) and updates the cask in `albertcbrown/homebrew-tap` with `TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN`, a token scoped to the tap and held only in the `release` environment. It stays inert until `ARD_VERSIONING` is `on` and that token exists, and on a tag push it releases only the newest tag, so a backfill publishes once. Gatekeeper: the binary is not yet signed or notarized, so the cask clears the quarantine flag after install (`hooks.post.install`), an accepted risk; notarization is a follow-up.\n201:\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"github: %s: %w: %s\", op, ErrForbidden, msg)\n643:\tif !errors.Is(err, ErrForbidden) {\n650:func (h *HTTP) RequiredChecks(ctx context.Context, branch string) (RequiredChecksResult, error) {\n690:\t\t\treturn RequiredChecksResult{}, nil\n692:\t\treturn RequiredChecksResult{}, err\n694:\tres := RequiredChecksResult{Available: true, Checks: normalizeChecks(items)}\n712:\t\t\tout[i] = CheckRun{Name: r.GetName(), AppID: r.GetApp().GetID(), Status: r.GetStatus(), Conclusion: r.GetConclusion(), ID: r.GetID(), CompletedAt: r.GetCompletedAt().Time}\n761:func (h *HTTP) AppID(ctx context.Context, slug string) (int64, error) {\n242-\tIntegrationID int64 // 0: any source may report it\n243-}\n244-\n245:// RequiredChecksResult is what the rules API says about a branch.\n246-//\n247-// Available is false when the repository has no rules API to ask: a free-plan\n248-// private repository (HTTP 403 \"Upgrade to GitHub Pro or make this repository\n249-// public\") or a 404. That is a normal answer meaning \"no required checks\", not\n250-// a failure. Available is true when the rules were read; Checks may then be\n251-// empty (no required_status_checks rule applies). Any other failure (5xx,\n252-// network, timeout, a permissions 403, an unexpected status